le the comma separation automatically. For negative values, I need to preserve the sign and add it before the $.\n\nLet me trace through some test cases:\n- 4640039 → \"$4,640,039\" ✓\n- -12345 → \"-$12,345\" ✓\n- 0 → \"$0\" ✓\n- -0.5 → This gets trickier since decimals should probably be handled too\n\nThe function assumes integers or floats with reasonable precision for currency display. I'll use abs() to format the magnitude, then prepend the appropriate sign.\n\nFor negative inputs, I need \"-$\" followed by the formatted value. For non-negative inputs, just \"$\" works. The key is ensuring the comma separators appear correctly regardless of whether the number is positive or negative.\n\nLet me test with various inputs to verify: -12345 becomes \"-$12,345\", and 4640039 becomes \"$4,640,039\". For zero, it should output \"$0\". Negative values should preserve their sign in the final string.\n\nThe simplest solution handles both cases cleanly: check if n is negative, apply the minus sign prefix accordingly, then use Python's built-in comma formatting on the absolute value.",
